1
00:00:24,077 --> 00:00:26,260
Greetings, peace and joy.

2
00:00:26,321 --> 00:00:28,285
I trust you had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:28,304 --> 00:00:32,090
I do not say happy, though I also wish you did have a happy day.

4
00:00:33,252 --> 00:00:49,798
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodasa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodasa

5
00:00:50,621 --> 00:01:03,518
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Harahato Sambha Sambuddhasa Homage to the Buddha, the Blessed Noble, and fully self-awakened one.

6
00:01:07,822 --> 00:01:15,912
Can I just say that if you've emailed me some questions and I haven't answered them, could you do me a favor and email them again?

7
00:01:16,873 --> 00:01:20,037
Probably, I've probably...

8
00:01:20,659 --> 00:01:25,084
Archive them carelessly or whatever.

9
00:01:26,146 --> 00:01:34,635
And, you know, don't feel afraid to ask a question that you think is silly.

10
00:01:35,115 --> 00:01:36,498
There are no silly questions.

11
00:01:37,379 --> 00:01:39,760
And I don't have to mention your name anyway.

12
00:01:41,183 --> 00:01:42,403
So it can be quite anonymous.

13
00:01:43,105 --> 00:01:50,093
And if you have a personal problem which you want to talk to me about, I'm quite happy to Skype.

14
00:01:50,596 --> 00:01:57,084
And I'll ask your permission if I can discuss it publicly.

15
00:01:57,566 --> 00:01:59,608
Of course, I don't have to mention your name at all.

16
00:01:59,709 --> 00:02:02,272
So it remains completely anonymous.

17
00:02:03,674 --> 00:02:04,114
Okay.

18
00:02:04,575 --> 00:02:13,306
So we were still answering this question about the Four Noble Truths and slowly making our way to the Eightfold Path.

19
00:02:14,929 --> 00:02:19,776
And yesterday we talked about the Buddha's first efforts to teach there.

20
00:02:20,211 --> 00:02:41,527
meeting the Ajivaka, not the Ajivaka, it'll come to me in time, meeting that wanderer and not really impressing him, and then finding his five companions not really wanting to speak to him,

21
00:02:41,947 --> 00:02:51,840
being rude, thinking he'd gone soft, and very slowly opening up with his question of, have you ever heard me speak like this before?

22
00:02:52,219 --> 00:03:06,018
And now I want to follow that through with the discourse called the Dhamma Chakravartana Sutta, which is translated as setting in motion the wheel of the Dhamma.

23
00:03:06,438 --> 00:03:08,921
So here the teaching is more formalized.

24
00:03:08,981 --> 00:03:11,245
It was put together obviously later.

25
00:03:11,596 --> 00:03:19,465
but it's still understood traditionally to be his first talk given to the five companions.

26
00:03:21,206 --> 00:03:38,986
Before I go on that, I just want to tell you a story which illustrates hagiography, how people build up in their imagination somebody whom they think is saintly,

27
00:03:39,389 --> 00:03:46,718
just through passing messages from one to another, what is called Chinese whispers.

28
00:03:46,778 --> 00:03:51,344
I'm not so sure that's PC anymore, but that's what we call it.

29
00:03:51,406 --> 00:03:53,669
I think in the States they call it telephone or something.

30
00:03:54,870 --> 00:03:58,955
And, of course, the same happens with anybody.

31
00:03:59,195 --> 00:04:02,580
If you talk about somebody, they pass it on to somebody else.

32
00:04:02,661 --> 00:04:05,564
Before you know it, they're either a criminal or a saint.

33
00:04:06,337 --> 00:04:13,063
So it so happened I was up in Kandy and went to visit Bhikkhu Bodhi up there.

34
00:04:14,585 --> 00:04:18,208
Occasionally, very occasionally, I would go up and visit him.

35
00:04:18,908 --> 00:04:27,396
And there was a Danish monk with him, whose name, of course, I'd forgotten, whom I hadn't known.

36
00:04:28,177 --> 00:04:34,843
And what had happened was that he was living in the forests

37
00:04:35,144 --> 00:04:38,108
in Sri Lanka, in a three-sided hut.

38
00:04:38,488 --> 00:04:41,572
I mean, he was living there with a three-sided hut.

39
00:04:41,593 --> 00:04:46,220
That means that one side was completely open to tarantulas, snakes, et cetera, et cetera.

40
00:04:46,781 --> 00:04:48,963
And he's very well known for that.

41
00:04:49,103 --> 00:04:52,829
And I do believe there are other monks now living in a similar way.

42
00:04:54,072 --> 00:05:01,843
When he was coming back from Armstrong, which would have been in the morning, early morning, I suppose, from the local villages,

43
00:05:02,228 --> 00:05:05,430
an elephant suddenly shot out and ran over him.

44
00:05:06,351 --> 00:05:11,456
Now, if an elephant, I'm told, if an elephant wants to kill you, they will definitely kill you.

45
00:05:12,216 --> 00:05:15,819
But in this case, it seems they just wanted to shock him or something.

46
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:24,267
But unfortunately, one of his feet landed on this monk's hip and shattered it, of course.

47
00:05:24,987 --> 00:05:27,971
And when I saw him, his leg was still up in the air.

48
00:05:28,031 --> 00:05:28,971
He'd been to hospital.

49
00:05:29,012 --> 00:05:30,192
They tried to do their best.

50
00:05:30,663 --> 00:05:34,168
Hopefully he's had it better seen too.

51
00:05:34,427 --> 00:05:34,848
Who knows?

52
00:05:36,149 --> 00:05:40,817
Anyway, I had visited Colombo just before this.

53
00:05:40,836 --> 00:05:42,057
It was on my way up, you see.

54
00:05:42,098 --> 00:05:58,240
So I used to stay at the International Buddhist Center there, which was established by the Bandranayakas, who were both presidents of the, well, prime ministers at that time of Sri Lanka, especially built for the Maharshi Sayadaw when he visited

55
00:05:58,793 --> 00:06:03,576
in about 1956 or something, maybe a bit later, who knows.

56
00:06:04,117 --> 00:06:09,283
And there was a Swedish monk whom I knew there, a friend.

57
00:06:10,584 --> 00:06:13,065
And again, I've forgotten his name.

58
00:06:13,726 --> 00:06:18,110
And he told me a story about this Danish monk, you see.

59
00:06:18,151 --> 00:06:19,853
He said, have you heard about it?

60
00:06:19,892 --> 00:06:20,492
I said, no.

61
00:06:21,574 --> 00:06:26,538
He said, well, he said he was sitting in lotus under a tree meditating.

62
00:06:26,923 --> 00:06:34,598
and a she-elephant passed with a baby, a little baby elephant, a cub or what I normally call a baby elephant, a baby elephant.

63
00:06:35,019 --> 00:06:43,875
And the baby elephant was drawn to this figure under a tree and approached it and tried to sit in lotus, fell over and broke his hip.

64
00:06:45,216 --> 00:06:51,327
So you can see what happens when stories are passed along the lines.

65
00:06:51,915 --> 00:06:55,480
As the story goes, he actually laid there all night.

66
00:06:56,081 --> 00:07:02,048
It was only in the next morning that the villagers recognized he hadn't turned up his arms around and went looking for him.

67
00:07:03,490 --> 00:07:13,601
So quite a telling story there about the way we build stories around things.

68
00:07:14,762 --> 00:07:21,872
So putting that aside, let me start this particular discourse.

69
00:07:22,156 --> 00:07:24,920
And as I go through, I'll try and make some sort of comments.

70
00:07:25,422 --> 00:07:26,944
So thus have I heard.

71
00:07:27,024 --> 00:07:41,007
Remember, these were all remembered by his attendant, Ananda, who became an Arahant just before the meeting, the big meeting that happened after he died.

72
00:07:41,814 --> 00:07:48,264
And it says that on one occasion, the Blessed One was dwelling at Varanasi in the deer park of Isipatana.

73
00:07:48,285 --> 00:07:55,696
So this is the place near present-day Varanasi on the Ganges there, in the deer park at Isipatana.

74
00:07:56,937 --> 00:07:59,081
I think it's now known as Saunat.

75
00:08:00,382 --> 00:08:03,728
There, the Blessed One addressed the bhikkhus as a group of five, thus.

76
00:08:04,389 --> 00:08:08,295
So he's now with his companions, his old companions, yeah.

77
00:08:08,646 --> 00:08:14,154
He says, because these two extremes should not be followed by one who has gone forth into homelessness.

78
00:08:15,637 --> 00:08:16,177
What to?

79
00:08:17,079 --> 00:08:27,115
The pursuit of sensual pleasure, of sensual happiness in sensual pleasures, which is low, vulgar, the way of worldlings, ignoble, unbeneficial.

80
00:08:27,755 --> 00:08:33,625
And the pursuit of self-mortification, which is painful, ignoble and unbeneficial.

81
00:08:34,229 --> 00:08:52,051
without veering towards either of these extremes, that the targeter has awakened to the middle way, which gives rise to vision, which gives rise to knowledge, which leads to peace, to direct understanding or direct experience, to enlightenment and to libana.

82
00:08:52,491 --> 00:09:03,325
Now, these two extremes, so by one who's gone forth into homelessness, so he's obviously talking to people who are,

83
00:09:03,557 --> 00:09:26,966
wandering mendicants um but here we can translate this as anybody who's on a spiritual path right this isn't just for monks and nuns this is for anybody who is on a spiritual path uh wanting to be released from all suffering so what to so he says the pursuit of sensual pleasure sensual happiness excuse me in sensual pleasure

84
00:09:27,402 --> 00:09:31,626
So remember, it's not the object itself that we want.

85
00:09:32,128 --> 00:09:35,392
It's not the good food, the sex, drugs and rock and roll.

86
00:09:35,773 --> 00:09:41,159
It's how we personally feel happy, excited when we have those things.

87
00:09:42,520 --> 00:09:49,029
He says this is low, you see, vulgar, the way of worldlings, ignoble and unbeneficial.

88
00:09:49,350 --> 00:09:50,471
So we have to stop it.

89
00:09:50,892 --> 00:09:53,014
That's the message there.

90
00:09:53,034 --> 00:09:55,158
In fact, it's more than unbeneficial.

91
00:09:55,357 --> 00:09:56,919
It produces addiction, doesn't it?

92
00:09:57,591 --> 00:10:05,724
That's the problem with depending on sensual pleasures in order to make ourselves happy.

93
00:10:06,004 --> 00:10:07,527
They become a dependency.

94
00:10:11,332 --> 00:10:16,581
And the next one is the pursuit of self-mortification, which is painful, ignoble and beneficial.

95
00:10:16,620 --> 00:10:20,647
Now, this self-mortification is really about blaming the body.

96
00:10:21,528 --> 00:10:26,316
It refers, I think, specifically to the practice of those days.

97
00:10:26,683 --> 00:10:34,854
The Jain leader was well known for this practice, and he was an elder contemporary of the Buddha.

98
00:10:35,434 --> 00:10:47,909
And there's some suggestion that the Buddha might have spent some time, or at least visited him or whatever, but he was definitely practicing self-mortifications for years, it's said.

99
00:10:48,510 --> 00:10:51,033
And the main thing, of course, was to draw the body down.

100
00:10:51,514 --> 00:10:54,057
Draw the body down, not...

101
00:10:54,307 --> 00:10:56,110
not indulge in any of the senses.

102
00:10:56,190 --> 00:10:59,573
He said he got so thin he could hold his spine through his stomach.

103
00:10:59,933 --> 00:11:02,836
So if you try that, you can see he's very, very, very thin.

104
00:11:03,697 --> 00:11:10,085
And the understanding was that it's the body that's at fault.

105
00:11:11,385 --> 00:11:14,149
You wouldn't have lusty thoughts if you didn't have eyes.

106
00:11:15,049 --> 00:11:17,993
You start blaming the body for your problems.

107
00:11:18,332 --> 00:11:22,677
You wouldn't be greedy if the body didn't have appetite.

108
00:11:22,860 --> 00:11:28,086
So you start working with the body to draw down its sensual pleasures.

109
00:11:28,866 --> 00:11:37,315
And that way you stop these reactions which lead you to more suffering.

110
00:11:37,335 --> 00:11:40,697
But he, of course, found this to be painful.

111
00:11:40,918 --> 00:11:42,158
Well, it's painful, of course, yes.

112
00:11:42,980 --> 00:11:46,743
Ig noble, so it wasn't leading on the spiritual path.

113
00:11:46,842 --> 00:11:50,606
Whenever the word aria is used, it's always about the spiritual path.

114
00:11:51,126 --> 00:11:52,308
And it was unbeneficial.

115
00:11:52,369 --> 00:11:53,429
He didn't get anything from it.

116
00:11:53,570 --> 00:11:55,393
In fact, he said it was just more suffering.

117
00:11:57,475 --> 00:12:01,961
There was, of course, a philosophy that underpinned this idea.

118
00:12:02,462 --> 00:12:06,107
And that was that the body itself was your karma.

119
00:12:06,748 --> 00:12:15,900
And that if you could just let go of the body, just let it go, the soul would rise naturally to an eternal heavenly place.

120
00:12:17,163 --> 00:12:18,804
And this definitely is true.

121
00:12:18,970 --> 00:12:32,318
The philosophy that underpins Jainism and a saint in Jainism is one who stops eating, basically, and often very elderly people will do so with that understanding.

122
00:12:34,264 --> 00:12:37,230
So the Buddha didn't didn't think that was beneficial.

123
00:12:37,551 --> 00:12:37,671
Right.

124
00:12:39,743 --> 00:12:45,910
Now, when he says the middle path, you see, it's not in between sensual pleasure and self-mortification.

125
00:12:46,251 --> 00:12:51,619
It's not you can have a bit of sensual pleasure and a bit of self-mortification and you'll find the way out.

126
00:12:51,639 --> 00:12:52,299
No, it's above.

127
00:12:52,581 --> 00:12:54,964
It's transcendent of it, right?

128
00:12:55,224 --> 00:13:01,231
The middle path has nothing to do with either the path of self-indulgence or the path of self-aversion.

129
00:13:01,852 --> 00:13:08,282
So this word middle has to be understood as a path which is transcendent of self.

130
00:13:08,836 --> 00:13:11,620
of these other two unbeneficial paths.

131
00:13:15,206 --> 00:13:19,615
And then he says, well, what is the middle way awakened to by the Tathagata?

132
00:13:19,654 --> 00:13:26,966
Remember that the word Tathagata means, translates as thus gone, which isn't particularly helpful.

133
00:13:26,986 --> 00:13:27,769
It's transcendent.

134
00:13:27,808 --> 00:13:31,114
He's gone, in his own words, he's gone beyond.

135
00:13:32,216 --> 00:13:34,320
Gone beyond.

136
00:13:35,988 --> 00:14:03,868
and he's a way that what the so he says what because is that middle way awakened to by the targeter which leads to nibana it is this noble eightfold path with right view right intention right speech right action right livelihood right effort right mindfulness and right concentration this because is the middle way awakened to by the targeter which gives rise to vision so uh

137
00:14:04,101 --> 00:14:15,658
which gives rise to knowledge, a direct understanding, leads to peace, to direct understanding, to enlightenment or awakening, and to nibbana.

138
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:32,280
And then now he gives us just these basic definitions of the Four Noble Truths, which are just worth reading through and also understanding how we should approach them.

139
00:14:32,301 --> 00:14:33,282
So the first one is,

140
00:14:33,667 --> 00:14:36,831
This is the noble truth of suffering.

141
00:14:36,851 --> 00:14:51,333
So remember that word dukkha has a whole meaning going from the smallest dissatisfaction, irritation, to the highest and most terrible sufferings that we're capable of.

142
00:14:52,394 --> 00:14:58,203
And it's also an existential suffering, which comes to us when death approaches.

143
00:14:59,346 --> 00:15:00,788
So birth is suffering.

144
00:15:01,105 --> 00:15:04,327
Aging is suffering illness is suffering death is suffering.

145
00:15:04,607 --> 00:15:14,376
So there we have it is it the union with what is displeasing is suffering and Separation from what is pleasing is suffering.

146
00:15:14,437 --> 00:15:23,426
So Getting some having to deal with something that we don't like and having Not to be able to get what we do like.

147
00:15:23,566 --> 00:15:30,251
I mean these are sufferings for people for those who are committed to trying to find some sort of

148
00:15:30,839 --> 00:15:32,620
permanent happiness in this life form.

149
00:15:33,741 --> 00:15:36,885
And then finally, not to get what one wants, of course.

150
00:15:36,966 --> 00:15:37,846
So we've done that.

151
00:15:38,427 --> 00:15:42,312
And the five aggregates subject to clinging are suffering.

152
00:15:42,793 --> 00:15:47,278
Now, we haven't got time to go into the five aggregates this evening, but some other time.

153
00:15:47,758 --> 00:15:52,884
So these five aggregates really refer to what we would call the psychophysical organism.

154
00:15:53,365 --> 00:15:56,067
And this is what we identify with.

155
00:15:56,087 --> 00:15:59,951
And that identity is, of course, the root cause of our suffering.

156
00:16:02,732 --> 00:16:07,837
Now, this because is the noble truth of the origin of suffering or the cause of suffering.

157
00:16:08,418 --> 00:16:16,225
It is this craving that leads to renewed existence, accompanied by delight and lust, seeking delight here and there.

158
00:16:17,047 --> 00:16:22,991
It is the craving for sensual pleasures, craving for becoming, craving for annihilation.

159
00:16:23,852 --> 00:16:28,857
So this the self or.

160
00:16:29,750 --> 00:16:34,333
our general motivation is to keep living.

161
00:16:34,433 --> 00:16:37,956
You know, like we want to wake up tomorrow morning, for instance.

162
00:16:38,918 --> 00:16:45,864
And we're chasing this business of pleasure and trying to get rid of what is unpleasure.

163
00:16:46,484 --> 00:16:49,647
And that's what this whole desire is concerned with.

164
00:16:50,187 --> 00:16:56,212
So the word tanha translates as desire or sometimes craving, which is a bit strong.

165
00:16:56,732 --> 00:16:59,115
It can be a craving, of course.

166
00:16:59,432 --> 00:17:07,083
But there's no English word which gets across the idea of a desire which is constantly unwholesome.

167
00:17:07,624 --> 00:17:09,086
And that's what tanha refers to.

168
00:17:09,386 --> 00:17:13,071
There is another word for desire, chanda, which can be either good or bad.

169
00:17:13,412 --> 00:17:23,205
But tanha is a specific word which refers to those desires that are unwholesome for us, whether we're wanting or not wanting.

170
00:17:25,347 --> 00:17:26,470
And so...

171
00:17:26,669 --> 00:17:32,836
The more fundamental desire, of course, is to keep living, to keep becoming, to keep being me.

172
00:17:33,717 --> 00:17:42,425
And the desire to exterminate or to commit suicide, basically, is the opposite of that.

173
00:17:43,146 --> 00:17:51,296
In a more gentle way, the desire to become is always to put myself first, to make sure that I'm okay.

174
00:17:51,615 --> 00:17:52,676
That's how it manifests.

175
00:17:52,977 --> 00:17:59,483
And the desire to annihilate can be as simple as not being able to handle something and just falling asleep.

176
00:18:00,003 --> 00:18:11,895
And that's some of the problems of dauntless and lethargy we get in our meditation is based upon that attitude, not wanting to be with anything that's painful or upsetting.

177
00:18:12,195 --> 00:18:13,017
So you fall asleep.

178
00:18:13,938 --> 00:18:17,922
So sleep becomes a way of sort of suppressing it, keeping it out of consciousness.

179
00:18:22,356 --> 00:18:24,820
So that's the second noble truth.

180
00:18:25,141 --> 00:18:27,904
The third noble truth is the cessation of suffering.

181
00:18:28,286 --> 00:18:33,432
It is the remainderless fading away and cessation of that same craving.

182
00:18:34,213 --> 00:18:38,760
The giving up and relinquishing of it, freedom from it, non-reliance on it.

183
00:18:39,141 --> 00:18:50,237
So that craving, that tanha, that unwholesome wanting, that unskillful wanting, not wanting, that's what has to go.

184
00:18:50,538 --> 00:19:04,714
And a word for it would be reactivity, just reacting to things, not responding from wisdom, not responding from a position where we understand, but always reacting from this deluded place.

185
00:19:05,415 --> 00:19:09,240
Now, we don't do that always, obviously, but often we do.

186
00:19:09,981 --> 00:19:12,924
And what we're doing is reinforcing an old habit about.

187
00:19:13,625 --> 00:19:16,108
It's so easy to fall into old habits.

188
00:19:16,609 --> 00:19:17,810
Old habits die hard.

189
00:19:20,035 --> 00:19:24,240
But in a sense, that sounds so simple, just get rid of this wrong desire.

190
00:19:24,681 --> 00:19:27,986
But of course, what we discovered is that that is quite a challenge.

191
00:19:28,165 --> 00:19:33,153
It's more than a lifetime's work, if you believe in rebirth.

192
00:19:35,435 --> 00:19:44,047
And finally, the noble truth of the path leading to the cessation of this suffering is the Eightfold Path.

193
00:19:45,048 --> 00:19:46,109
And just...

194
00:19:46,579 --> 00:19:51,306
just quickly before we begin, because I want to do the Eightfold Path finally tomorrow.

195
00:19:51,586 --> 00:19:53,229
I'm absolutely determined.

196
00:19:54,691 --> 00:20:03,001
The Noble Truth of Suffering, he says, having stated what it is, he says this is what has to be fully understood.

197
00:20:03,021 --> 00:20:04,144
You have to understand it.

198
00:20:05,325 --> 00:20:14,577
And it's not just basic intellectual understanding, of course, but a direct understanding through personal experience.

199
00:20:15,352 --> 00:20:18,336
And part of that is the process of meditation, vipassana.

200
00:20:19,438 --> 00:20:25,228
And then he says, as far as I'm concerned, this has been fully realized, fully understood, excuse me.

201
00:20:27,250 --> 00:20:34,923
The noble truth of suffering, the origin of it, he says, has to be abandoned.

202
00:20:35,743 --> 00:20:38,127
This is what we have to abandon completely.

203
00:20:38,588 --> 00:20:42,034
And then he goes on to say that he has, in fact, abandoned it.

204
00:20:43,262 --> 00:20:54,233
The noble truth of end of suffering has to be realized, a direct personal experience of an Ibanic moment.

205
00:20:54,755 --> 00:20:58,298
And he says that, of course, he has actually experienced that too.

206
00:21:03,282 --> 00:21:07,686
And then, of course, the path itself is something that is to be developed.

207
00:21:08,248 --> 00:21:11,411
And, of course, he goes on to say that this has been developed.

208
00:21:12,051 --> 00:21:13,252
So that's the process.

209
00:21:13,586 --> 00:21:18,833
of becoming fully liberated from suffering.

210
00:21:18,952 --> 00:21:34,088
So what I would like to do, I've just got time, I'd like to skip over to the ending of this discourse, which then shows you how it's built up into these wonderful images of other worlds, other gods, et cetera, et cetera.

211
00:21:35,351 --> 00:21:42,278
So long as Bhikkhus, as my knowledge and vision of these four noble truths as they really are in their three phases,

212
00:21:42,528 --> 00:22:04,955
and 12 aspects, that's what we've just been through, was not thoroughly purified in this way, I did not claim to have awakened to the unsurpassed perfection, to the unsurpassed perfect enlightenment in this world, with its devas, gods, Mara and Brahma, those are the higher gods, and in this generation of ascetics and Brahmins, its devas and humans.

213
00:22:05,037 --> 00:22:09,362
Devas are the beings around us.

214
00:22:10,539 --> 00:22:23,058
But when my knowledge and vision of the Four Noble Truths, as they really are in their three phases and twelve aspects, was thoroughly purified in this way, then I claim to have awakened to the unsurpassed perfect enlightenment in this world.

215
00:22:24,481 --> 00:22:26,845
Unshakable is the liberation of my mind.

216
00:22:27,165 --> 00:22:28,567
This is my last birth.

217
00:22:29,108 --> 00:22:32,374
Now there is no more renewed existence.

218
00:22:32,394 --> 00:22:35,198
This is the knowledge and vision that arose in me.

219
00:22:36,712 --> 00:22:42,903
This is what the Blessed One said and elated the bhikkhus of the group of five, delighted in the Blessed One's statement.

220
00:22:43,443 --> 00:22:51,497
And while this discourse was being spoken, there arose in the Venerable Kondanya the dust-free, stainless vision of the Dhamma.

221
00:22:52,117 --> 00:22:56,785
Whatever is subject to a beginning is subject to an end.

222
00:22:57,185 --> 00:22:59,650
Everything arises and passes away.

223
00:23:00,675 --> 00:23:07,303
And when the wheel of the Dhamma had been set in motion by the Blessed One, the earth-dwelling Devas raised a cry.

224
00:23:07,963 --> 00:23:22,539
At Baranasi in the deer park of Isipatana, the unsurpassed wheel of the Dhamma has been set in motion by the Blessed One, which cannot be stopped by any ascetic or Brahmin or Deva or Mara or Brahma or anyone in the world.

225
00:23:23,201 --> 00:23:29,788
And having heard the cry of the earth-dwelling Devas, the Devas of the realms of the four kings raised the same cry.

226
00:23:30,747 --> 00:23:48,942
And from there to the Tapatimsa Devas and the Yama Devas, the Tusita Devas, the Nimanarati Devas, and the Devas of the Brahma's company raised the cry at Varanasi in the deer park at Isipatana.

227
00:23:49,383 --> 00:23:52,890
This unsurpassed wheel of Dhamma has been set in motion.

228
00:23:53,815 --> 00:24:01,742
by the Blessed One, which cannot be stopped by any ascetic or Brahmin or Deva or Mara or Brahma or anyone in that world.

229
00:24:02,364 --> 00:24:22,084
Thus, at that moment, at that instant, at that second, the cry spread as far as the Brahma world, and this ten-thousand-fold world system shook, quaked and trembled, and an immeasurable glorious radiance appeared in the world, surpassing the divine majesty of the Devas.

230
00:24:23,296 --> 00:24:29,805
Then the Blessed One uttered this inspired utterance, Kondanya has indeed understood.

231
00:24:30,286 --> 00:24:32,528
Kondanya has indeed understood.

232
00:24:32,990 --> 00:24:38,278
And in this way, the Venerable Kondanya acquired the name of Anya Kondanya.

233
00:24:38,817 --> 00:24:40,981
Kondanya, the one who knows.

234
00:24:43,525 --> 00:24:52,999
So that's a lovely poetic remembrance of the effect of turning the wheel of the Dhamma for us poor human beings.

